    "description": "Four-sided limestone column, one of the V&A's foremost Buddhist stone carvings, set at the side of a road in southern Shaanxi province in north China. A group of 26 Buddhist believers paid for the column, which bears their names carved in rows. The main carved decoration is of the Buddha on one side and of the religious debate between the Bodhisattva Manjusri (Wenshu) and the pious lay-believer Vimalakirti (Weimojie). The inscription on the stone reads: 'so that all wayfarers may gaze upon the radiant countenance, without having to turn aside their steps…'",
